body:
Opening a document of a few kilobytes freezes WordCraft for tens of seconds to minutes. For every **distinct** character that no loaded font covers, layout re-reads and parses every system font file from disk. A 2 KB file with 500 Tangut characters froze the desktop app for 45 s here, and the cost grows linearly with the number of distinct characters.

Follow-up to #59: the per-character fallback cache from #233 doesn't help when every character is different.

Reported here at @vangel76's request on Discord.

## Affected

- All releases (0.1.0 to 0.5.0) and `main` at `1d4f741`; the code path exists since `d1e80df`.
- Native builds: desktop app, `wordcraft-cli`, MCP server. Web is not affected (`cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))`).
- Any input format (.txt, .docx, .rtf…), and PDF export.

## Root cause

`FontDb::system_fallback()` (`crates/fonts/src/fontdb.rs:1008`): when no preferred fallback family covers `c`, it walks the whole catalog and, for each file under 40 MB, reads the entire file and parses its charmap:

```rust
// crates/fonts/src/fontdb.rs:1033
for p in paths {
    if std::fs::metadata(&p).map(|m| m.len() > 40 << 20).unwrap_or(true) {
        continue;
    }
    let Ok(data) = std::fs::read(&p) else { continue };
    let hit = enumerate_faces(&data).iter().any(|f| skrifa::FontRef::from_index(&data, f.index).is_ok_and(|f| f.charmap().map(c).is_some()));
    ...
}
self.sys.lock().unwrap_or_else(|e| e.into_inner()).misses.insert(c);
```

The miss is only remembered for that one character, so N distinct uncovered characters cost N full scans of every system font, on the layout thread. Unicode has tens of thousands of assigned characters most systems have no font for (about 6,900 Tangut ideographs alone, more in the CJK extensions).

